**When to choose an interface vs. an abstract class**

| Decision factor | Interface | Abstract class |
|-----------------|-----------|----------------|
| **Multiple inheritance** | A class can implement any number of interfaces, so use an interface when you need to combine several unrelated capabilities. | A class can extend only one abstract class, so use it only when you need a single inheritance hierarchy. |
| **Behavior vs. contract** | Interfaces describe *what* a type can do (method signatures) without providing any implementation (except default methods in Java 8+). | Abstract classes can provide both *what* and *how* – they can contain concrete fields, constructors, and method implementations. |
| **Versioning** | Adding a new method to an existing interface breaks all implementers (unless you use default methods). | Adding a new concrete method to an abstract class does not affect subclasses. |
| **State** | Interfaces cannot hold instance state (no fields, except static constants). | Abstract classes can hold protected fields, constructors, and shared state. |
| **Domain modeling** | Use when you want to define a *role* that many unrelated classes can play (e.g., `Refundable`, `Auditable`). | Use when you have a clear “is‑a” relationship and want to share code (e.g., a base `PaymentProcessor`). |

---

### Payment‑processing example

```java
// 1️⃣ Interface: the contract that any payment handler must fulfil
public interface PaymentGateway {
    // Send a payment request and get a result
    PaymentResult process(PaymentRequest request);

    // Optional: cancel a pending transaction
    void cancel(String transactionId);
}

// 2️⃣ Abstract class: common code shared by most gateways
public abstract class AbstractPaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    // Shared configuration (e.g., API credentials)
    protected final String apiKey;
    protected final String endpoint;

    protected AbstractPaymentGateway(String apiKey, String endpoint) {
        this.apiKey = apiKey;
        this.endpoint = endpoint;
    }

    // A helper that many concrete gateways can reuse
    protected HttpResponse post(String path, String body) {
        // ... generic HTTP POST implementation ...
    }

    // The concrete `process` method is left abstract – each gateway
    // implements its own request/response handling.
    @Override
    public abstract PaymentResult process(PaymentRequest request);
}
```

#### When to use the **interface** alone
* You have a completely different payment system that does **not** share any code with the others (e.g., a legacy SOAP service).  
* You need a class that already extends another class (e.g., `class MobileAppPayment extends SomeFrameworkClass implements PaymentGateway`).  
* You want to define a *role* such as `Refundable` that unrelated classes can adopt.

#### When to use the **abstract class** (or extend it)
* Most of your gateways need the same configuration handling, logging, and HTTP helper – you don’t want to duplicate that code.  
* You want to enforce that every gateway provides a `process` implementation while still giving them shared utilities.  
* You anticipate adding new **concrete** methods (e.g., `protected void logRequest(...)`) that won’t break existing subclasses.

#### Combined usage (common pattern)

```java
public class StripeGateway extends AbstractPaymentGateway {
    public StripeGateway(String apiKey) {
        super(apiKey, "https://api.stripe.com");
    }

    @Override
    public PaymentResult process(PaymentRequest request) {
        // Build Stripe‑specific JSON, call post(), parse response, etc.
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(String transactionId) {
        // Stripe‑specific cancellation logic
    }
}
```

Here `StripeGateway` **inherits** shared state/behaviour from `AbstractPaymentGateway` **and** satisfies the `PaymentGateway` contract. If a new payment provider cannot reuse the HTTP helper, it can implement `PaymentGateway` directly without extending the abstract class.

---

**Bottom line:**  
- Use an **interface** when you only need a contract or when a class must inherit from another superclass.  
- Use an **abstract class** when you have common implementation, shared state, or want to provide utility methods that many concrete gateways will reuse.

*Later, revisit Java’s default methods and multiple inheritance of types so you can decide when an interface alone can also carry reusable code.*

